+## 1. Placement tests & the staircase
+
+| # | Finding | Status |
+|---|---|---|
+| 1.1 | The rule matches a classic *transformed up-down* staircase. See 1.2. | 🟡 |
+| 1.2 | **Confirmed in the literature.** A rule that makes the level go up after two correct and down after one miss converges on about **70.7% correct** (Levitt 1971). So a student settles where they answer roughly 7 in 10 correctly — higher than a "50/50" level and not the same as "mastered". Sources: [Levitt 1971 summary](https://typeset.io/papers/transformed-up-down-methods-in-psychoacoustics-31x0jr24ab), [lecture notes](https://engineering.purdue.edu/~ece511/LectureNotes/pp12.pdf). The app's docs should say so. | 🟢 |
+| 1.3 | Levitt-style methods estimate the threshold from the **mean of the reversal levels**; the app uses the **final level** instead. No rule on how many reversals or items are enough appeared in the snippets (check Levitt's paper). | 🟡 |
+| 1.4 | No accuracy data can exist without your own pilot. See "Studies you can run" on the agenda. | ⚪ |
+| 1.5–1.7, 1.10–1.12 | Nothing specific found; these need your own item data and expert review. | ⚪ |
+| 1.6 | Dutch school levels differ (A2 expected for havo/vwo year 1–2 vs A1 for vmbo-bb), which supports starting at a level that follows the class — see 4.1. | 🟡 |
+| 1.8 | Pelánek (2016) is the standard review of Elo in education and notes the usual improvement of an **uncertainty function** (smaller steps as an item gathers responses) instead of a fixed K ([paper](https://www.fi.muni.cz/~xpelanek/publications/CAE-elo.pdf)). The app uses a fixed K = 24. A 2026 follow-up says shrinking-K schemes are inadequate in some situations. | 🟢 |
+| 1.9 | Nothing specific found on cut-offs. | ⚪ |
+
+## 2. Grading with rubrics
+
+| # | Finding | Status |
+|---|---|---|
+| 2.1 | Jonsson & Svingby (2007), a review of 75 studies, links more reliable rubric scoring to **analytic criteria, task-specific wording, exemplars and rater training**; validity got less attention. Brookhart & Chen (2015) updated it. See [summary](https://frontiersin.org/journals/education/articles/10.3389/feduc.2018.00022/full). *(Secondary summaries only.)* | 🟢 |
+| 2.4 | **Comparative judgement** often shows higher reliability than rubric marking at similar time cost, but adaptive pairing may **inflate** reliability estimates, and reliability alone doesn't prove validity. See [Cambridge Assessment paper](https://www.cambridgeassessment.org.uk/Images/296241-the-reliability-of-adaptive-comparative-judgment.pdf), [JoWR](https://www.jowr.org/index.php/jowr/article/view/867). | 🟢 |
+| 2.5 | Hattie & Timperley (2007): feedback averages a large effect (0.79) but varies a lot; feedback on the **task, process and self-regulation** helps more than praise about the self. A later re-analysis of 435 studies found a medium effect (d = 0.48) with high variation ([Wisniewski et al. 2020](https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2019.03087/full)). The 2007 summary table has also been criticised. | 🟡 |
+| 2.8 | Peer vs teacher ratings correlate about **.63** on average across 69 studies (Li et al. 2016), ranging roughly .33–.86 depending on context; the effect of anonymity and training needs the full paper ([overview](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10648-019-09510-3)). | 🟡 |
+| 2.2, 2.3, 2.6, 2.7, 2.9, 2.10 | Not searched in this pass. 2.3 (mark conversion for Dutch VO) and 2.6 (late penalties) are good next targets. | ⚪ |
+
+## 3. Question types & scoring
+
+| # | Finding | Status |
+|---|---|---|
+| 3.1 | Studies on multiple-response scoring generally find **partial-credit rules perform better psychometrically than all-or-nothing**, including Ripkey's rule and balanced rules; the differences are sometimes small. The app's rule (credit for correctly *unselected* options too) is a form of "balanced" scoring but gives a non-zero score to a blank answer — compare it with other rules. See [Psychometrika framework](https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/psychometrika/article/psychometric-model-framework-for-multiple-response-items/10D6DCD514D67281E4695FFC00C88B6A) and the 2021 EPM paper "Evaluating Different Scoring Methods for Multiple Response Items Providing Partial Credit" (DOI 10.1177/0013164421994636). | 🟢 |
+| 3.6 | The **C-test** (damaged words) is designed around "reduced redundancy" and general proficiency; the **rational-deletion cloze** gives the author control over *content* but is *not* a measure of general proficiency (Grotjahn, quoted). Fixed-ratio cloze results depend on text, deletion rate and starting point. Construct validity of the C-test is still debated, and one study suggests it also reflects processing speed ([overview](https://research.bangor.ac.uk/portal/files/78069213/28Jul2020The_C-test_as_predictor_final_manuscript.pdf)). The app's gap generators should be labelled accordingly. | 🟢 |
+| 3.2–3.5, 3.7–3.11 | Not searched in this pass. | ⚪ |
+
+## 4. CEFR, mastery & learning paths
+
+| # | Finding | Status |
+|---|---|---|
+| 4.1 | See the section at the top. | 🟢 |
+| 4.5 | The app's index is built from open lists; the **CEFR-J wordlist** (Tono) is free for research and commercial use with citation and is based on a different evidence base (textbook corpora) than the Cambridge **English Vocabulary Profile**. Disagreement between lists is expected; a direct EVP–CEFR-J comparison study was not found. Leads: [Tono's appraisal of the EVP](https://www.ceeol.com/search/chapter-detail?id=837579), [CEFR-J data](https://github.com/openlanguageprofiles/olp-en-cefrj). | 🟡 |
+| 4.6 | **Flesch-Kincaid is a rough proxy.** It mostly reflects sentence and word length, was built for native readers, and several studies and a 2025 *SSLA* article question its construct validity for L2 materials. One study found sentence word count the strongest predictor. Use alongside vocabulary profiling. See [Cambridge SSLA article](https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/DB604DB02A205F0F172D6024137CBFE8/S0272263125101125a.pdf/aligning-linguistic-complexity-with-the-difficulty-of-english-texts-for-l2-learners-based-on-cefr-levels.pdf). | 🟢 |
+| 4.2–4.4, 4.7–4.11 | Nothing found yet; 4.3 and 4.4 need your data, 4.7 needs a parser-accuracy-on-learner-text study. | ⚪ |
+
+## 5. Flashcards & spaced repetition
+
+| # | Finding | Status |
+|---|---|---|
+| 5.1 | The FSRS scheduler comes from Ye, Su & Cao (KDD 2022), which reports a 12.6% improvement over earlier methods, using data from a language-learning app (MaiMemo) — adult self-study, not classrooms ([paper listing](https://scholar.hit.edu.cn/en/publications/a-stochastic-shortest-path-algorithm-for-optimizing-spaced-repeti/)). No classroom or teenager study found. | 🟡 |
+| 5.2 | The FSRS project provides an "optimal retention" method that balances workload against knowledge ([FSRS wiki](https://github.com/open-spaced-repetition/fsrs4anki/wiki/The-optimal-retention)). Whether 90% is the default needs checking in the library source. | 🟡 |
+| 5.3–5.5 | Not searched. | ⚪ |
