+## 7. Fairness & integrity
+
+| # | Finding | Status |
+|---|---|---|
+| 7.1 | **Read-aloud is contested.** The main worry is that it turns a *reading* test into a *listening* test (Sireci, NAEP review). Evidence is mixed and mostly small studies; some US test consortia banned it for early grades while others allowed it. Human readers vary in quality; computer voices standardise delivery but one tiny study found worse comprehension. The app's choice — teacher enables it per student, and a badge is shown on results — is consistent with the cautious approach. See [Sireci](https://www.nagb.gov/content/dam/nagb/en/documents/publications/conferences/sireci.pdf), [JTLA article](https://ejournals.bc.edu/index.php/jtla/article/download/1660/1496). | 🟢 |
+| 7.2 | **Almost no independent validation exists** for proctoring flags. A preprint says accuracy and false-positive rates of commercial tools haven't been published in peer-reviewed work; vendors' accuracy claims are unsupported; flags are not conclusive proof of cheating, and a stray notification can trigger a tab-switch flag ([preprint](https://www.researchsquare.com/article/rs-10540699)). This supports showing flags as *information for the teacher*, never as a verdict. | 🟢 |
+| 7.3–7.5 | Not searched. | ⚪ |
+
